From e939ea0f3b7bcb98bbacc61f03117166f83046fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bvchess <53128914+bvchess@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 09:23:30 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 13/14] The ghost search bars x from the picks and forces it in
 the parity solve alone (Prover::with_parity_forcing, which replaces
 with_parity_of: numbers picked in every parity solve whatever a node's roles
 say, with the parity bound's own weights, left out of the set the node
 branches on and reports).  The private factor of the previous version had to
 be smaller than x, since the matching takes the larger end of an edge as the
 pick, and at 2067 x is the smallest source, so the forced picks could not be
 matched there and the search ended at its root; with x barred no second board
 is needed.  The example prints the root's matching and parity bounds and
 checks a set found for playability and for independence at both parities with
 x

---
 solver/noframe/examples/ghost.rs | 83 ++++++++++++++++++--------------
 solver/noframe/src/game.rs       | 29 ++++++++---
 2 files changed, 69 insertions(+), 43 deletions(-)

diff --git a/solver/noframe/examples/ghost.rs b/solver/noframe/examples/ghost.rs
index 0eca59f..f7d42d1 100644
--- a/solver/noframe/examples/ghost.rs
+++ b/solver/noframe/examples/ghost.rs
@@ -11,21 +11,22 @@
 //! m/2 free (no bottom-layers roles: they are a theorem about the game's optimal sets, not about this question), and the
 //! target opt(m), so that a set beats it exactly when its promotions weigh more than P\*(m).
 //!
-//! * `ghost` (the default): the search runs on a copy of the board on which x lists one private factor, a source of
-//!   board m outside U\*(m) whose Ω has the other parity than x's (above m/2, it is no number's maximal factor, and it is
-//!   barred): there x always pays at once, so every playability test, conflict and matching of the search is that of
-//!   B ∪ L on the true board, x left out.  The parity bound is built from the true board (`with_parity_of`), so x keeps
-//!   its true vectors there, forced.  The answer to (T).
+//! * `ghost` (the default): x is barred from the picks, so every playability test, conflict and matching of the search
+//!   is that of B ∪ L, and the target is opt(m) − x; the parity bound picks x in every solve, with weight 0
+//!   ([`Prover::with_parity_forcing`]), so the sets it counts are independent at both parities together with all of
+//!   U\*(m).  The answer to (T).
 //! * `plain`: the true board throughout.  No set beats P\*(m), since m is proven (given U at m): a check of the search.
 //! * `hole`: the true board with x barred and the target opt(m) − x: the game of the board without x with the sources
 //!   fixed to B (E1's question).  E1 found sets above P\*(m) at every game: a check that the search finds what is there.
 //!
-//! A set found is checked again on the true board: the playability of its members less x (ghost) or of its members
-//! (plain, hole), by the board's peel, and its weight.  One JSON line: the verdict, the nodes, the time and the set.
+//! A set found is checked again: the playability of its members by the board's peel, its weight, and its independence
+//! at both parities (with x in the ghost mode) by a parity solve with every member picked.  One JSON line: the root's
+//! bounds, the verdict, the nodes, the time and the set.
 
